index c9a7c533..63f75029 100644
--- a/docs/shiploop/architecture.md
+++ b/docs/shiploop/architecture.md
@@ -43,13 +43,13 @@ As of f6c46b59. The repository keeps no decision records and no architecture doc
 - Contracts linked, not copied: Management API reference `templates/docs/api.md`; settings reference `templates/docs/self_hosted_configuration.md`; container notes `docker/README.md`.
 
 ## Decisions
+Request sentences are cited as `Basis: S<k>`: S1 = move notification delivery out of the sendalerts process into Celery workers with a Redis broker, so that delivery can scale across several worker machines; S2 = keep every existing integration working. `(accepted)` marks a decision made by a plan and not yet implemented.
+
 - AD-1 (inherited) One Django project with one app per feature area (accounts, api, front, logs, payments) and one app per integration kind under `hc/integrations`, each listed in `INSTALLED_APPS` and routed from `hc/urls.py`. Why: it keeps each feature area and each integration kind self-contained, so a kind can be copied or removed as a unit (inferred). Evidence: `hc/settings.py` `INSTALLED_APPS`; `hc/urls.py` `urlpatterns`.
 - AD-2 (inherited) The user interface is server-rendered templates with small jQuery scripts; pages update by polling endpoints that return HTML fragments or JSON, with no single-page app, build step, websocket or server push. Why: self-hosting stays simple because nothing needs building or serving beyond Django itself (inferred). Evidence: `templates/base.html` `jquery-3.6.0.min.js`; `static/js/adaptive-setinterval.js` `adaptiveSetInterval`.
 - AD-3 (inherited) All state lives in one relational database reached through the ORM, on SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL or MariaDB alike; schema changes are numbered migrations per app; code must not rely on engine-specific behaviour, and query code is written to avoid known slow plans (sort by `created`, not `id`). Why: the README names these database families as the supported building blocks, and the same code and tests must run on all of them (stated: `README.md` "PostgreSQL, MySQL or MariaDB"). Evidence: `hc/settings.py` `DATABASES`; `.github/workflows/tests.yml` `matrix`.
-- AD-4 (inherited) Alert delivery is a pull model with no broker: the web process (or the alert process itself) writes a status change row, and the separate `sendalerts` process polls for unprocessed rows and delivers them; there is no scheduler and no retry table. Why: the web process only records the change, and the separate alert process picks it up and processes it, using the same rows for downtime statistics (stated: `hc/api/models.py` "picks it up and processes it"). Evidence: `hc/api/models.py` `create_flip`; `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` `process_one_flip`.
 - AD-5 (inherited) Work is claimed with optimistic conditional UPDATEs that must change exactly one row (a status change is claimed by setting its processed time where it is still empty; a check goes down by an update guarded on its old status; reports claim by their next date), so several processes can run without locks. Why: several worker processes may share one database, and a lost race is simply skipped (stated: `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` "another sendalerts process got there first"). Evidence: `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` `handle_going_down`; `hc/api/management/commands/sendreports.py` `handle_one_report`.
 - AD-6 (inherited) Delivery is at most once per status change and integration: the status change is marked processed before any integration is contacted, a failed attempt is recorded and never re-queued, and an escaped exception or crash abandons the remaining integrations of that status change; retries exist only inside one attempt. Why: marking the work done first prevents duplicate alerts from concurrent workers or restarts, at the price of no recovery after a crash (inferred). Evidence: `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` `num_updated`; `hc/api/transports.py` `request`.
-- AD-7 (inherited) `sendalerts` is one long-running management command: a poll loop with a 2 second idle sleep, a thread pool of `--num-workers` (default 1), signal handlers that let running deliveries finish, stale DB connections closed per thread, and the DB application name set to the command; operators must keep it running, and the Docker image stops the container when any child exits. Why: alerts are a continuous duty of an instance, so operators must supervise the process and containers must fail loudly when it dies (stated: `README.md` "Running in Production"). Evidence: `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` `handle`; `docker/run.sh` `wait -n`; `CHANGELOG.md` "v5.0 - 2026-10-20".
 - AD-8 (inherited) One transport class per integration kind, registered in the `TRANSPORTS` table in `hc/api/models.py` and imported on first use; a transport implements `notify(flip, notification)` and, when it can ignore a status, `is_noop(status)`; HTTP kinds extend `HttpTransport` and Slack-compatible kinds extend `Slackalike`. Why: a uniform contract lets the alert process treat every kind alike and keeps each kind's code in its own app (stated: `CONTRIBUTING.md` "Developing a New Integration"). Evidence: `hc/api/models.py` `TRANSPORTS`; `hc/api/transports.py` `HttpTransport`.
 - AD-9 (inherited) Delivery errors are the typed exception `TransportError(message, permanent)`: the message is user-facing text that is stored and shown verbatim; `permanent` disables the integration and ends in-attempt retries; any other exception is treated as a bug and propagates out of the attempt. Why: it separates expected remote failures, which users must see, from bugs, which go to the log, and lets a transport say "stop trying" (inferred). Evidence: `hc/api/transports.py` `TransportError`; `hc/api/models.py` `transports.TransportError`.
 - AD-10 (inherited) One delivery attempt is one `Notification` row, inserted before sending with the error text "Sending" and finalized afterwards; later callbacks address the same row through its random public `code`; the `Channel` columns `last_notify`, `last_notify_duration` and `last_error` are a denormalized summary of the latest attempt, overwritten each time. Why: the row must exist before sending so provider callbacks and bounces can name it and so in-progress attempts are visible (inferred). Evidence: `hc/api/models.py` `n.error = "Sending"`; `hc/api/views.py` `notification_status`.
@@ -70,6 +70,18 @@ As of f6c46b59. The repository keeps no decision records and no architecture doc
 - AD-25 (inherited) Tests run with Django's runner, one `tests/` package per app; CI runs them on SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL and MariaDB with Python 3.12 to 3.15, plus `mypy --strict` and a coverage run; `TransactionTestCase` appears once, where a test needs real commits and an `IntegrityError`. Why: the supported-environment promises are only proven by running the suite on each of them (inferred). Evidence: `.github/workflows/tests.yml` `matrix`; `hc/api/tests/test_notify.py` `TransactionTestCase`.
 - AD-26 (inherited) Static typing is enforced: every module starts with `from __future__ import annotations` and the tree must pass `mypy --strict` with the django-stubs plugin; formatting is ruff. Why: the style section asks for ruff and simplicity, and the strict type check runs in CI on every push (stated: `CONTRIBUTING.md` "Code Style"). Evidence: `.github/workflows/mypy.yml` `mypy --strict`; `mypy.ini` `django-stubs`.
 - AD-27 (inherited) User-visible changes are written into `CHANGELOG.md` under the top "## v..." heading, and the application's displayed version is read from the first such heading at start-up. Why: the changelog is the release record and the running version is derived from it (stated: `CHANGELOG.md` "All notable changes"). Evidence: `hc/settings.py` `VERSION`; `CHANGELOG.md` "Unreleased".
+- AD-28 (accepted) Delivery is queue-based: `sendalerts` keeps detecting overdue checks and claiming status changes, then enqueues one Celery task per claimed status change on a Redis broker, and Celery workers on any number of machines deliver. The web process still only writes `Flip` rows and never touches the broker. Why: S1 asks for delivery in Celery workers on a Redis broker so it scales across machines; keeping the flip rows and the claim reuses AD-5 and the downtime statistics. Alternatives: several `sendalerts` processes on several machines (already possible through AD-5; the minimal option I would have preferred; rejected because S1 asks for Celery); enqueue from the web process when the flip is created (rejected: couples the ping path to the broker and to transaction timing); one task per integration (rejected: breaks the delivery-duration order of R-1 and the single log block per status change, and is not needed to scale across status changes). Basis: S1. Replaces AD-4.
+- AD-29 (accepted) `sendalerts` is a poll loop that detects overdue checks, claims status changes and enqueues them; it keeps the 2 second idle sleep, the SIGTERM and SIGINT handlers (stop taking work) and the DB application name, and owns no thread pool or semaphore; `--num-workers` is still accepted but ignored with a warning, as `--pool` is today. Delivery concurrency belongs to the worker (`--concurrency`) and to the number of workers. Why: S1 moves delivery out of the process; rejecting the old argument would break existing launch scripts on upgrade. Alternatives: remove `--num-workers` (rejected: breaks scripts); keep the thread pool as a no-broker fallback (rejected: contradicts S1, Assumptions). Basis: S1. Replaces AD-7.
+- AD-30 (accepted) Layout and names: the Celery app is `hc.celery:app`, configured from Django settings with the `CELERY_` namespace; the task and the moved delivery function live in `hc/api/tasks.py`, which imports `hc.api.models` (`Flip`), `hc.lib.statsd` and `hc.celery`, is imported by `sendalerts` (which calls `deliver_flip`) and found by the worker's autodiscovery, and is never imported by `hc.api.models`; workers start with `celery -A hc.celery worker`; the web process and every other command never import Celery. Names do not collide: the module name `hc.celery` is safe because imports are absolute and nothing runs with `hc/` on `sys.path`, the task name is `hc.api.tasks.deliver_flip`, the queue is `healthchecks` (AD-31), and no `CELERY_*` setting exists yet. Why: AD-1 and a web process that works without a broker. Alternatives: the documented recipe that imports the app in `hc/__init__.py` and uses `shared_task` (rejected: every process, including web workers and tests, would import Celery); leaving `notify` in the command module (rejected: workers would import a management command, and `sendalerts` importing the task would be circular). Basis: S1.
+- AD-31 (accepted) Task contract: one task, `deliver_flip(flip_id)`; the payload is the status-change id only, JSON is the only accepted serializer, results are ignored, and the queue is named `healthchecks` so a shared Redis does not mix tasks with another Celery application; the worker re-reads the flip, check, integrations and settings, and a missing flip is a no-op. No inbound endpoint is added; the broker is inside the operator's trust boundary, and a hostile message can at worst re-trigger delivery for an existing status change, never supply content or code. Why: S1 introduces the broker as a new input channel and the payload must not carry stale data, secrets or code. Alternatives: pass the flip's fields in the message (rejected: stale data, personal data in Redis); pickle (rejected: code execution); a result backend (rejected: nothing reads results). Basis: S1.
+- AD-32 (accepted) Delivery semantics stay at most once (AD-6): early acknowledgement (late acks off), no automatic retries, one prefetched task per worker slot so a slow delivery does not hold tasks other workers could take. The claim stays before the enqueue; if the publish fails with the broker-connection error (kombu `OperationalError`), `sendalerts` restores `processed=None` for that flip, logs the error on the `hc` logger and lets the loop's 2 second sleep pace the retry, so a broker outage delays alerts instead of losing them; broker connect and socket timeouts are bounded to seconds so an unreachable Redis cannot stall overdue-check detection. A publish whose outcome is ambiguous may duplicate one alert, which is accepted. Why: S1 adds a failure source (the broker) that AD-6's crash-only model does not cover, and undoing the claim is the smallest repair with the existing mechanism. Alternatives: leave the flip claimed and lose the alert (rejected: a Redis outage would drop every alert raised during it); claim in the worker (rejected, AD-5); a database outbox with re-enqueue of stale claims (the stronger design, needs new state, Assumptions). Basis: S1.
+- AD-33 (accepted) Redis is only the broker: no result backend, cache or session store; its keys are the ones kombu manages (the `healthchecks` list and bookkeeping keys), the application defines none; no migration or schema; messages are transient and may be lost with the Redis data, as AD-6 already allows for crashes. The reference compose runs Redis with append-only persistence on a named volume and no published port; operators give the broker its own Redis database number and add a password or TLS (`rediss://`) as needed. Why: S1 requires a Redis broker; anything more would add state no sentence asks for. Alternatives: RabbitMQ or the Django database as broker (rejected: S1 names Redis). Basis: S1.
+- AD-34 (accepted) Configuration: `CELERY_BROKER_URL` is an environment variable read through `envsecret` (so `CELERY_BROKER_URL_FILE` works) with the default `redis://localhost:6379/0`; the fixed options of AD-31 and AD-32 are set in `hc/settings.py` under the `CELERY_` namespace and can be overridden in `hc/local_settings.py`; both variables are documented in the settings reference; the `manage.py test` override block turns on eager execution with propagation, so tests and CI need no Redis; no system check or startup failure is added because the web process and the tests must start without a broker and the existing check tests assert exact id lists. Why: AD-18 and AD-25. Alternatives: a `REDIS_URL` name translated in code (rejected: extra mapping); failing when unset (rejected: breaks `runserver` and tests). Basis: S1.
+- AD-35 (accepted) Observability in the worker keeps AD-19: the per-status-change lines ("<code> goes <status>" and one line per integration) go to the worker's process log at INFO through a logger outside the `hc` hierarchy, so the database handler does not store a row per delivery; unexpected exceptions are logged on the `hc` logger (rows carry the worker's host name) and re-raised so Celery records the failure too; the statsd names are unchanged, with the delivery ones (`hc.notifications.*`, `hc.sendalerts.dwellTime`, `hc.sendalerts.sendTime`) emitted by workers, `hc.sendalerts.processFlip` staying with the claim, and the dwell time now including queue wait; the metrics endpoint is unchanged (`num_unprocessed_flips` counts status changes not yet claimed). Why: S1 moves the code that emits them. Alternatives: store the delivery lines as `hc.logs.Record` rows (rejected: a row per alert); add a queue-depth field to the metrics endpoint (rejected: a Redis read in the web process that no sentence asks for). Basis: S1.
+- AD-36 (accepted) Worker environment: workers run the same release, settings and environment variables (and `hc/local_settings.py`) as the web and `sendalerts` processes (`SECRET_KEY`, `SITE_ROOT`, database, SMTP, S3, integration credentials), reach the same database and Redis, and have the outbound network access `sendalerts` had, so every integration kind runs unchanged in whichever worker takes the alert. Machine-local integrations run on the worker's machine: Shell commands with the worker's permissions, and Signal through `SIGNAL_CLI_SOCKET` (a UNIX socket must exist on every worker machine, a TCP `host:port` works from any); there are no per-kind queues or routing. Workers on several machines need PostgreSQL, MySQL or MariaDB, since SQLite is one machine. Why: S1 puts workers on several machines and S2 keeps every kind working. Alternatives: per-kind queues with routed workers (rejected: needs one task per integration). Basis: S1, S2.
+- AD-37 (accepted) Reference deployment: `docker/run.sh` supervises one worker (Celery's default prefork pool, `--concurrency=1` to match the former `--num-workers` default, `--loglevel=INFO` so the delivery lines stay visible) beside `sendalerts` and `sendreports`, and the container still exits when any child exits; more capacity is more worker processes or containers started from the same image with `celery -A hc.celery worker`; compose gains a Redis service and a commented-out `worker` service; a local checkout runs `runserver`, `sendalerts` and a worker in separate shells beside a Redis. Why: single-container installs keep working with one extra service, the default concurrency stays as it was, and a dead worker fails loudly as AD-7's container-exit rule did. Alternatives: a worker-only compose service enabled by default (rejected: a single `docker run` install would stop delivering); Celery's thread pool (rejected: less used than prefork); Celery's default concurrency of one slot per CPU (rejected: a silent change of default). Basis: S1.
+- AD-38 (accepted) Typing: `celery.*` and `kombu.*` are listed in `mypy.ini` with `ignore_missing_imports` and the task decorator carries a targeted `type: ignore`, as the repository already does for `statsd`; no stub package is added unless the pinned release ships usable types. Why: AD-26 without a new development dependency. Alternatives: `celery-types` in `requirements-dev.txt` (rejected: a third-party stub package to track). Basis: none.
 
 ## Superseded
-None. The repository keeps no decision records, so no record is superseded (linked decision records: 0).
+- AD-4 (superseded) Alert delivery is a pull model with no broker: the web process (or the alert process itself) writes a status change row, and the separate `sendalerts` process polls for unprocessed rows and delivers them; there is no scheduler and no retry table. Why: the web process only records the change, and the separate alert process picks it up and processes it, using the same rows for downtime statistics (stated: `hc/api/models.py` "picks it up and processes it"). Evidence: `hc/api/models.py` `create_flip`; `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` `process_one_flip`. Basis: S1. Replaced by AD-28.
+- AD-7 (superseded) `sendalerts` is one long-running management command: a poll loop with a 2 second idle sleep, a thread pool of `--num-workers` (default 1), signal handlers that let running deliveries finish, stale DB connections closed per thread, and the DB application name set to the command; operators must keep it running, and the Docker image stops the container when any child exits. Why: alerts are a continuous duty of an instance, so operators must supervise the process and containers must fail loudly when it dies (stated: `README.md` "Running in Production"). Evidence: `hc/api/management/commands/sendalerts.py` `handle`; `docker/run.sh` `wait -n`; `CHANGELOG.md` "v5.0 - 2026-10-20". Basis: S1. Replaced by AD-29.
